# `and`, `or`, `mod` and `is` are legal dictionary keys, so after a complete entry value a worded
# operator is genuinely ambiguous. `spec/grammar.md:314`'s **Entry lookahead** rule settles it by
# the token that follows, *"ignoring any line breaks between the two — so a dictionary written on
# one line and the same dictionary written across several lines always read alike"* (issue #944,
# maintainer ruling option 4; this file was written for #933's narrower rule and is re-derived here).
#
# The discriminator is the separator's LEXEME, not its position and not adjacency:
#
#   - a `colon` token opens the next entry           -> `mod: 2`, `mod : 2`, `and: 3`
#   - a `variable` token is a variable-read          -> `mod :two`, `mod:two` stay OPERATORS
#
# Both readings parse cleanly, so reading either one wrongly changes the dictionary *silently*
# instead of raising a diagnostic. Each entry is read back so the reading is proven, not assumed —
# a dict's printed form does not show its entries.
define two
  return 2
end

# A *variable* named `two` as well, so the operator readings below have something to read.
:two = 2

# Colon separators: each of these opens the next entry, and the multi-line spelling must agree with
# the one-line spelling of the same dictionary.
:spaced = { a: 1
mod: 2
and : 3
or:4 }
print :spaced.a
print :spaced.mod
print :spaced.and
print :spaced.or

:one_line = { a: 1 mod: 2 and : 3 or:4 }
print :one_line.a
print :one_line.mod
print :one_line.and
print :one_line.or

# Variable reads: `mod :two` and `mod:two` keep the word an OPERATOR, so each of these dictionaries
# has ONE entry. Under #933's superseded rule they opened entries keyed `mod`; reading them that way
# now would silently reparse a valid program.
#
# `is :two` belongs to this group and is deliberately NOT here: as an operator it is an
# `is-predicate` missing its form word, so it raises `ol-bad-token` and cannot appear in a fixture
# that executes. It is pinned in `packages/parser/src/multiline-expressions.test.mjs` instead.
#
# The next two dictionaries are read back TWICE, and the second read-back is the discriminating one.
# This corrects a claim an earlier revision of this comment made — that no primitive could read the
# entry set back, so `.a` was the best check available. `keys` does exactly that, and review caught
# it. `.a` alone cannot discriminate: `1 mod :two` is `1 mod 2` = 1, and under the superseded
# reading `a` is 1 as well, so both readings print the same. `keys` shows the entry SET, which is
# precisely where the two readings differ. The operator reading is pinned additionally in
# `packages/parser/src/multiline-expressions.test.mjs:346-358`.
:glued = { a: 1
mod:two }
print :glued.a
print keys :glued

:split = { a: 1
mod
:two }
print :split.a
print keys :split

# With no separator after it, the same word is an operator continuing the value above: this dict
# has ONE entry whose value is `5 mod 3`. The guard is scoped to the dictionary that owns the
# newline, so inside a nested list or group the word is an operator again — and these use
# `mod :two`, which reads BOTH ways without a diagnostic, so they detect a leaked guard.
:continued = { a: 5
mod 3 }
print :continued.a

:nested = { a: [1
mod :two] }
print :nested.a

:grouped = { a: (1
mod :two) }
print :grouped.a
